{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790603093,"nonce":"W4abYFkfBzUXeDOJsMEjd0paPHw=","title":"Language-neutral host attachments for foreign object faces","message":"The remaining Haxe-to-Wren construction path creates three allocations: the Haxe face, the Wren object, and a cell linking them. The face is allocated before the Wren object exists, so the object-tail mechanism used by the opposite crossing cannot remove this cell.\n\nAdd a language-neutral host-attachment seam. A selected foreign face holds its core object directly and the core object keeps a weak pointer back to that face. The foreign collector must notify Caribou when the face dies so the weak pointer can be cleared without finding a dead face through the surviving object.\n\nThe seam must not encode Wren in Ash or in the core heap:\n\n- Ash describes per-class allocation policy: extra tail bytes and whether the class carries a host attachment requiring cleanup. Subclasses inherit the policy.\n- Ash passes the Haxe type and policy flags to the host object allocator. Compiled allocation sites use the host allocator only for selected classes; ordinary Haxe allocation remains inline.\n- Caribou allocates those objects with a distinct conservatively traced foreign-object kind carrying a drop bit.\n- A drop-safe protocol operation releases a holder language's weak front. It may not allocate or take the heap lock.\n- The adapter implements release with an atomic exact-pointer comparison so an old face cannot clear a replacement.\n- Cleanup checks whether the attached object survived the same collection before dereferencing it. If both objects died, no action is needed.\n\nUse the seam first to remove the Haxe-to-Wren construction cell. The Haxe face's Ref field points strongly to the Wren allocation. The Wren bridge word stores a tagged weak face pointer. A later Wren-to-Haxe crossing reuses a live face or creates and binds a replacement after cleanup.\n\nAcceptance:\n\n- selected classes and subclasses receive exactly one cleanup notification;\n- ordinary Haxe classes keep the existing allocation path and receive none;\n- a face keeps its Wren object alive;\n- a Wren object does not keep its face alive;\n- crossing preserves identity while the face lives and creates a valid replacement after it dies;\n- both unreachable objects are reclaimed without a leak;\n- cleanup remains valid under GC stress and when either side is swept first;\n- hosted paths that do not reserve attachment metadata continue using cells;\n- the wasm interop construction benchmark records the resulting cost.\n\nRelated: ccaa3bfd43b22a5b35323868af510ab98d54c20f17478b6e5e4ddee2879255d8 and eb28555.","files":null}]}